<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajaraja Chola III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1216">1216</a>–<a href="/years/1256">1256</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajaraja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> III was king of the declining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a> from 1216 to 1256. Under him the Chola kingdom was overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, and around 1231 he was captured by the Kadava chief Kopperunchinga, being freed only through the intervention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Narasimha II. The Cholas thereafter depended on Hoysala protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajaraja-chola-iii.jpg" alt="Rajaraja Chola III" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajaraja3_territories.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rajaraja succeeded Kulothunga Chola III in July 1216, inheriting a kingdom much reduced in size and influence. With the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> in the south the Cholas had lost most of their lands beyond the Kaveri, and their hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> in the north was slipping as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> grew stronger. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Kalyani had given way to the Seunas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Cholas</a> held the Andhra country around Vengi. Chola feudatories seized the chance to transfer their allegiance, and the Kadava chieftains of Kudalur were quick to exploit their overlord's weakness.</p><p>Historians judge Rajaraja neither a warrior nor a statesman equal to the times. Pandyan inscriptions state that he broke the terms of his treaty with his Pandyan overlord and refused to pay tribute. A punitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> army entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> capital and Rajaraja fled, only to be caught and imprisoned at Sendamangalam by the Kadava Kopperunchinga I, a former Chola feudatory.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> had been allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> since the time of Kulothunga III, and Rajaraja had married a sister or a daughter of Vira Narasimha II. Hearing of the king's capture and of the devastation of the Chola country, Narasimha sent his army, which sacked two of Kopperunchinga's towns. As it prepared to besiege Sendamangalam, Kopperunchinga sued for peace and released the king. Narasimha himself led his troops against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and defeated them near Mahendramangalam on the Kaveri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>For the rest of his reign Rajaraja depended heavily on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> help. Order within the kingdom declined and the feudatories paid less and less heed to central authority, though the lands under his nominal rule stayed as they had been under Kulothunga III. Some historians read a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> inscription from Jambai, dated 1239 in Rajaraja's 23rd regnal year, as recording a victory of his subordinate Tikka I, a Nellore Chola ruler, in which a Hoysala king identified with Narasimha II was killed.</p><p>His brother and rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii">Rajendra Chola III</a>, began to take effective control of the administration while Rajaraja was still alive, and the epigraphs of Rajendra point to a civil war between the two. Among Rajaraja's officials was Mallan Sivan, also called Brahmadaraya muttaraiyan, who held a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and governed Urattur-nadu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajaraja III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Narasimha II intervened repeatedly to protect the weakened Chola king <b>Rajaraja III</b> against the Pandyas and the Kadava chief Kopperunchinga, and his son Vira Someshvara built a second capital at Kannanur Kuppam near Srirangam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1219 he invaded the Chola country, burned Thanjavur and Uraiyur and celebrated a victory coronation in the Chola heartland, forcing <b>Rajaraja III</b> to accept him as overlord; the Chola king was restored only through Hoysala intervention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
